## 2. Grant

The licensor grants you a copyright and patent license to use, study,
copy, modify, and redistribute the software (in source or compiled form,
in whole or in part), and to make and redistribute changes and new works
based on it, for any **Permitted Purpose** defined in Section 3.

## 3. Permitted Purpose

A "Permitted Purpose" is any of the following:

a. **Noncommercial use of any kind** — personal study, teaching,
   academic research, hobby or amateur projects, or any use without
   anticipated commercial application.

b. **Use by nonprofit or public institutions** — charitable
   organizations, educational institutions, public research
   organizations, public health/safety organizations, environmental
   organizations, or government institutions, regardless of how that
   institution is funded.

c. **Humanitarian use** — any use directed at public benefit rather
   than private financial gain.

d. **Contribution to open source formal-verification projects.**
   Reproducing, adapting, porting, or redistributing this software (in
   whole or in part) as a contribution to, or dependency of, any project
   distributed under an OSI-approved open source license — including,
   without limitation, Mathlib, Physlib, the Lean 4 core/stdlib, or any
   other open source proof assistant, theorem prover, or formal
   verification library — is a Permitted Purpose. This holds even if the
   upstream project is used, funded, or sponsored by a commercial
   organization, as long as the redistribution itself remains under an
   open source license and is not the basis of a separately sold,
   closed, or proprietary product.

## 4. Excluded Purpose — Commercial Use

Any use **not** covered by Section 3 is a "Commercial Purpose" and is
**not** licensed under these terms. A Commercial Purpose includes,
without limitation, incorporating this software or its direct output
(in whole or in part) into a proprietary, closed-source, or
commercially-licensed product — for example, a commercial computer
algebra system, symbolic-computation package, or any other paid software
— where the software or its output is sold, sublicensed, bundled, or
otherwise monetized outside of an open source license as described in
Section 3(d). Use for a Commercial Purpose requires a separate written
commercial license from the licensor, obtained in advance.

